/* Njia — course catalogue
 *
 * DATA PROVENANCE — two separate fields, on purpose.
 *
 * A single `data_confidence` flag used to cover a whole record. It did not:
 * every verification_note ever written here describes a FEE, while the same
 * record also carries `employment_rate` and `median_salary_kes`. The result
 * was invented outcome statistics rendering under a "Verified" badge, and
 * feeding the employment sort, the payback calculation, and the comparison
 * table's "better" winner. A teenager was being told one course beat another
 * on evidence that did not exist. Hence:
 *
 *   fees_confidence     — 'verified' only when total_fees_kes traces to a
 *                         named, dated source recorded in verification_note.
 *   outcomes_confidence — covers employment_rate and median_salary_kes.
 *
 * `outcomes_confidence` is 'illustrative' on every record, and that is not
 * laziness. Programme-level graduate outcome data is not published in Kenya:
 * government only recently began tracking TVET graduates for three years
 * post-completion, and no equivalent per-course series exists for universities.
 * A course-level "78% employment rate" is therefore not merely unsourced — it
 * is unsourceable today.
 *
 * These figures used to be kept as coarse planning assumptions, labelled as
 * estimates everywhere they appeared. That is no longer the position, and the
 * change is worth recording because the old one sounded careful enough to
 * survive several audits.
 *
 * Labelling qualified each number while leaving the DIFFERENCES between them
 * intact, and the differences were the whole fabrication. A learner reading
 * 88% against 45% takes that as a reason to choose one course over another,
 * and nothing sat underneath it — the spread was invented, so the comparison
 * it invited was noise wearing the clothes of evidence. An "(est.)" tag does
 * not fix a number that should not have a value.
 *
 * So employment_rate and median_salary_kes are now null on every record, and
 * a test enforces it. The fields are kept rather than deleted so the guards
 * written around them keep their meaning and a regression has something to
 * fail against.
 *
 * What a learner sees instead is sourced and coarser: ENTRY_PAY in
 * data/labour-market.js, a pay RANGE attached to a named role and employer
 * type — TSC CBA scales, published artisan day rates — surfaced per cluster
 * rather than per course. Less precise, and precise about how much it knows.
 * The sector-level anchor in SECTOR_EARNINGS carries its citation too.
 *
 * If a tracer study ever publishes real per-course outcomes, set
 * outcomes_confidence: 'verified', cite it, and put the numbers back — never
 * before, and never as an unsourced estimate again.
 */

/* Kenya Utalii College and its new Kilifi campus sit outside the consolidated
 * public-TVET fee. Both are state corporations under the Ministry of Tourism
 * and set their own charges, and the published figures do not agree with each
 * other, so one shared note carries the conflict rather than each record
 * asserting a number it cannot defend. */
/* KMTC is a Ministry of Health training corporation with its own national fee
 * structure, not a TVET institute on the consolidated rate. One record (c028)
 * was priced off the consolidated rate anyway and marked verified — the same
 * mistake as the Utalii one, at a different institution, and it survived
 * because nothing checked that a record's fee basis matched its institution's
 * fee regime. tests/sector-coverage.test.js now checks exactly that. */
const KMTC_FEE_NOTE = 'KMTC publishes one national fee structure across all campuses: Ksh 82,200 in Year 1 and Ksh 78,000 in each subsequent year for regular Kenyan students, 2025/26 structure (kmtc.ac.ke, cross-reported by Tuko, EduGuide and Education News Hub, August 2026). Total here is that schedule multiplied out by course length. Tuition only — accommodation, meals and skills-lab charges are extra and vary by campus. A non-refundable Ksh 1,500 application fee applies. Confirm with the campus before paying.';

const UTALII_FEE_NOTE = 'Kenya Utalii College is a state corporation under the Ministry of Tourism, NOT a TVET institute on the government\'s consolidated Ksh 67,189 rate, so that rate must not be used to derive its fees. Published Utalii figures conflict: roughly Ksh 38,850 per semester (about 77,700 a year), a quoted diploma range of Ksh 88,000-92,000, a certificate range of Ksh 80,000-85,000, and one one-year certificate quoted at Ksh 125,900. Njia will not choose among those and call the result verified, so the figure shown is an illustrative annual-equivalent scaled to course length and is flagged as such. Entry grades are from KUCCPS: a KCSE mean grade of C plain for diplomas and C- for certificates. Confirm the current fee and the exact programme list with the college or on the KUCCPS student portal before you decide.';

/* Fee notes for the parastatal and industry-owned institutes. Each is a state
 * corporation under a line ministry with its own schedule, so none may derive a
 * fee from the consolidated public-TVET rate. Where an institute publishes no
 * schedule the record carries null and the note says so — a missing figure a
 * learner can chase beats a plausible one they cannot. */
const PARASTATAL_NOTES = {
  easa: 'East African School of Aviation is the training directorate of the Kenya Civil Aviation Authority, a state corporation that sets its own fees rather than sitting on the consolidated public-TVET rate. Published 2026 figures give a diploma range of roughly Ksh 115,950 to 226,700 and IATA programmes from Ksh 125,900 to 197,700, with accommodation about Ksh 18,000 a month and a Ksh 1,000 application fee. The figure shown is the middle of the published diploma range because per-course fees were not published in the sources available here — confirm the exact fee for your programme with the school.',
  kac: 'Kenya Aeronautical College is a private, KCAA-approved school at Wilson Airport with its own published schedule. The Diploma in Aeronautical Engineering (Avionics) is published as Ksh 162,000 in Year 1, 141,000 in Year 2 and 143,000 in Year 3, a total of Ksh 446,000; the cabin crew certificate is published as Ksh 48,000 and 47,000 across two terms, a total of Ksh 95,000. Aviation training is among the most expensive routes in this catalogue and the fee is the main thing to plan against. Confirm with the college.',
  miog: 'Morendat Institute of Oil and Gas is owned by the Kenya Pipeline Company and operates as a centre of excellence for the energy sector, so it is not on the consolidated public-TVET rate. No fee structure was published in the sources available here, so no figure is shown rather than a guessed one — this institute publishes no fee schedule that could be verified. Ask MIOG directly, and ask specifically whether KPC sponsorship or industry attachment applies to your programme.',
  kengen_gtc: 'The KenGen Geothermal Training Centre at Olkaria is KenGen\'s centre of excellence and trains for geothermal operations across the region. No fee structure was published in the sources available here, so no figure is shown rather than a guessed one — this centre publishes no fee schedule that could be verified. Much of its provision is short-course and industry-sponsored, so ask about sponsorship before assuming you would pay privately.',
  kewi: 'The Kenya Water Institute is a state corporation established by the KEWI Act 2001, with campuses in Nairobi, Kitui, Tharaka Nithi and Kisumu, and sets its own fees. Entry is a KCSE C- with Mathematics and English for diploma programmes and around D+ for certificates. Published sources quote optional accommodation at about Ksh 25,960 a semester but no verifiable tuition schedule, so no tuition figure is shown rather than a guessed one — the institute publishes no fee structure that could be confirmed here. Ask the campus directly.',
  rti: 'The Railway Training Institute is a public TVET institution under the Ministry of Transport, training for railway, transport and logistics roles. Fee figures circulating online (roughly Ksh 8,000 to 14,000) are for one-to-four-week SHORT courses and must not be read as diploma fees; the institute does not publish a diploma or certificate fee schedule in any source available here, so no figure is shown rather than a wrong one. Confirm with the institute.',
  bandari: 'Bandari Maritime Academy is the Kenya Ports Authority\'s maritime training arm and central to the blue economy agenda, setting its own fees. Published sources give tuition of about Ksh 95,000 for a one-year module, and entry from a KCSE D plain to D+ depending on programme. The figure shown is that published module fee multiplied by course length. Maritime certification also carries separate statutory and medical costs — confirm the full picture with the academy.',
  kihbt: 'The Kenya Institute of Highways and Building Technology sits under the State Department for Roads and has trained for the roads sector since 1948, with its own fee schedule. Published figures are about Ksh 22,000 a term for diploma programmes and Ksh 19,500 a term for certificate and craft programmes, with accommodation and meals about Ksh 17,000 a term. The figure shown assumes three terms a year, which is the usual Kenyan TVET year but was not stated in the source, so it is illustrative — confirm the number of terms and the current rate with the institute.',
  kesra: 'The Kenya School of Revenue Administration is the Kenya Revenue Authority\'s own training school for tax, customs and fiscal policy, with its own published fees. The diploma programmes are published at Ksh 150,000 in total, with a non-refundable application fee of Ksh 2,000 for Kenyan applicants, and entry at a KCSE mean grade of C. Confirm with the school before paying.'
};

